entry.summary:
I like writing SQL. But in TypeScript, raw SQL usually means giving up types: you get any[] back, or you write the row type by hand and hope it stays in sync with the query.
So I built TypeSQL. It checks your SQL strings with the TypeScript compiler and gives you exact types for rows and parameters.
const rows = await sql(
  `SELECT u.email, p.title
   FROM users u LEFT JOIN posts p ON p.author_id = u.id
   WHERE u.id = ${sql.param(userId)}`,
).execute();
// rows: { email: string; title: string | null }[]

title is string | null because of the LEFT JOIN. userId must be a number, because it's compared to u.id. Nobody wrote those types: the compiler worked them out from the SQL.
